course: everyday
lesson: 500
title: The big sister: bossy, protective, borrowing your stuff
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Big sisters are often bossy and protective in the same morning. In Swansea, Megan-Lee, 15, bosses Cerys, 9, from breakfast on: «Shoes on. Not THOSE shoes. Brush your hair, you look like a scarecrow.» Then at the school gate a Year 6 boy pushes Cerys, and Megan-Lee is there in two seconds: «Touch my sister again and see what happens, butt» («butt» is South Wales for «mate»). Cerys is fuming at the bossing and glowing at the rescue. Both come from the same place: a big sister feeling responsible for you. You can tell her the bossing annoys you, and say thanks for the rescue.
explain: Borrowing without asking breaks trust. In Atlanta, Destiny, 16, sees her favourite hoodie, taken without asking, on her little sister Imani's TikTok: «Girl, you did NOT. That's my fit» («fit» = outfit). Then she says she'll borrow Imani's AirPods «for a week, no cap» («no cap» = no lie, for real). In Edinburgh, it goes the other way: Morven, 16, borrows Iona's glittery hair clips for a party: «Ah, they're braw, cheers, wee yin» («braw» = lovely, «wee yin» = little one). Iona is weirdly proud. Asking first, «Can I wear your hoodie Friday?», usually gets a yes and saves a war.
explain: Privacy matters. In Aberystwyth, Ffion, 9, reads her big sister Lowri's diary and finds out Lowri likes a boy called Iwan. At tea Ffion sings, «Lowri loves Iwan!» Lowri goes white, then cries, then doesn't speak to her for three days. Ffion thought it was funny; now she feels awful. A diary is private, and a secret found by snooping is not yours to use. In Toronto, Charlotte does Bea's makeup «for fun», then posts the photo without asking, and kids at school call Bea «clown face». Anyone, even a sister, should ask before posting your picture, and Bea can ask her to take it down.
explain: Secrets can be light or heavy. In Dublin, Clodagh, 17, lets Saoirse play on her phone, and a message pops up from someone Saoirse doesn't know: «u still sneaking out tonite?» Clodagh grabs the phone: «Not a word to Mam, right?» Small secrets, like a surprise present, you can keep. Secrets about danger are different: if you think your sister could get hurt, tell a grown-up, even if she's raging after. In Reading, Nomsa, 13, from Johannesburg, walks Thandi to school; one morning with her friends she says, «Walk behind us, now now.» In South Africa «now now» means «soon», and «just now» can mean «later» — confusing! Big sisters sometimes want to look cool in front of friends; it doesn't mean they don't love you.
explain: Big sisters can be grumpy and kind in the same hour. In Newcastle, Darcey asks Kayleigh, 15, for help with fractions: «Ugh, howay man, I'm busy» (Geordie for «oh come on»). At bedtime she helps for half an hour. In Wigan, Jodie, 16, buys Hollie a hot chocolate every Saturday with her part-time job money, until one week: «Can't, I'm skint» (no money). Hollie sulks, then feels bad: a treat is a gift, not a rule. In Bradford, with Mum in hospital for a week, Aziza, 17, cooks, does Maryam's plaits, checks homework, and cries quietly in the bathroom at night. Big sisters who do a parent's jobs need help too: make her a tea, and tell Dad or a teacher if she seems really down.
fact: Big sisters are often bossy and protective at the same time; both come from feeling responsible.
fact: You can tell a sibling the bossing annoys you and still thank her for looking out for you.
fact: Borrowing without asking breaks trust, even between sisters; asking first usually gets a yes.
fact: A diary is private; using a secret found by snooping to embarrass someone can wreck trust for a long time.
fact: After hurting someone, a real sorry and a promise never to do it again are the way back.
fact: Anyone, a sister included, should ask before posting a picture of you.
fact: Small harmless secrets can be kept; secrets about danger should be told to a grown-up.
fact: Teenagers sometimes want to look cool in front of friends; it does not mean they don't love you.
fact: Teenagers can be grumpy and kind within the same hour; asking at a good moment helps.
fact: A treat from a sibling is a gift, not a rule; say thanks, and don't sulk when it can't happen.
fact: A big sister doing a parent's jobs in hard times needs help and kindness too.
fact: «Butt» is South Wales for mate, «fit» means outfit, «no cap» means for real, «braw» is Scots for lovely, «wee yin» means little one, «howay» is Geordie for come on, «skint» means having no money, and in South Africa «now now» means soon.
exercise: Megan-Lee bosses Cerys all morning, then rescues her at the gate. Where do both come from? | Feeling responsible for her | Hating her little sister | Wanting to show off to boys | Being told to by Mum
exercise: What does «butt» mean in South Wales? | Mate | Bottom | Go away | Brother
exercise: How might Cerys feel after that morning? | Fuming and glowing at once | Only happy, all day | Only angry, for ever | Nothing at all
exercise: What did Imani do with Destiny's hoodie? | Wore it without asking, and it ended up on her TikTok | Asked politely first, and Destiny said yes straight away | Gave it to a charity shop because it was too big | Washed it and ironed it for her as a nice surprise
exercise: «That's my fit.» What is a «fit»? | An outfit | A tantrum | A gym | A fight
exercise: What usually saves a war over borrowing? | Asking first | Hiding your stuff | Locking your door | Borrowing back
exercise: What did Ffion do with Lowri's diary? | Read it and sang her secret at tea | Hid it to keep it safe from Mum | Wrote nice notes in it for her sister | Gave it back without opening it once
exercise: How did Lowri react? | Went white, cried, and didn't speak for three days | Laughed it off and sang along with her sister | Told Ffion it was fine and she didn't mind | Read Ffion's diary back the very next day
exercise: What is the way back for Ffion? | A real sorry and a promise never again | Telling Lowri it was only a joke | Buying her a new diary and saying nothing | Telling Iwan so it's all out in the open
exercise: What should Bea ask Charlotte? | To take the photo down | To post more photos | To do her makeup again | To tell her mates
exercise: Clodagh's phone shows «u still sneaking out tonite?» and she says «Not a word to Mam». What should Saoirse think? | If her sister could get hurt, tell a grown-up | Every secret must be kept, no matter what | Text the stranger back to ask who they are | Tell everyone at school about the message
exercise: In South Africa, what does «now now» mean? | Soon | This second | Never | Yesterday
exercise: Why does Nomsa tell Thandi to walk behind? | She wants to look cool with her friends | She has stopped loving her little sister | Thandi walks too slowly for anyone | It's a rule at the school in Reading
exercise: Kayleigh says «howay man, I'm busy», then helps at bedtime. What does that show? | Teens can be grumpy and kind in the same hour | Kayleigh was lying about being busy all along | She only helps when Mum is watching her | Fractions are easier to do late at night
exercise: Jodie says, «Can't, I'm skint.» What does that mean? | She has no money | She is too tired | She is ill | She doesn't care
exercise: What is a hot chocolate from your big sister? | A gift, not a rule | Something she owes you | A school rule | A bribe
exercise: Aziza cooks and does plaits while Mum's in hospital, and cries at night. What can Maryam do? | Make her a tea, help, and tell Dad if she seems really down | Ask her to do more plaits and cook bigger dinners | Pretend not to hear and keep out of her way | Tell her teenagers aren't allowed to cry
test: «my big sis is SO bossy, she defo hates me». What does Cerys's morning show? | Bossy and protective often go together | Bossy sisters never stick up for you | She should stop talking to her sister | Sisters who boss you around hate you
test: «it was just a joke, reading the diary was harmless lol». What's true? | Snooping and sharing a secret can wreck trust for a long time | It's fine because sisters aren't allowed to have secrets at all | It's harmless as long as you only read the first few pages | Diaries are meant to be read out loud at tea, that's the point
test: A big sister says, «Keep my secret: I'm meeting someone I met online tonight, don't tell Mum». What should a little sister do? | Tell a grown-up; it could be dangerous | Keep it, because sisters never tell on each other | Go with her to keep her company and stay quiet | Post about it so her friends can warn her
test: Iona's friend says, «Ugh, Morven took your clips, that's well rude». What makes it okay this time? | Morven asked and said thanks | Clips aren't real property | Iona is younger, so it doesn't count | Morven paid for them
test: What would probably happen if Destiny kept borrowing Imani's AirPods without asking? | More rows and less trust | They would become best friends | Mum would buy everyone AirPods | Nothing; sisters never mind
test: «big sis bought me cocoa every Sat and now she's stopped, so unfair». What does the lesson say? | A treat is a gift, not a rule | She has to keep buying it forever | Sulk until she buys two next week | Tell Mum to make her pay up
test: In a cartoon, the big sister posts embarrassing photos of her little sister and everyone laughs, the end. What's missing? | She should have asked first, and it hurt | Nothing; posting photos is always fine | The little sister should post one back | Makeup photos can't upset anybody
test: ★★★ Jodie earns money and spends £2 on Hollie's hot chocolate every Saturday. She stopped after 9 Saturdays, then started again and did 3 more. She says, «I've spent £20 on you!» Is she right? | No, it's £24 | Yes, exactly £20 | No, it's £18 | No, it's £12
test: ★★★ Nomsa says «now now» on Monday (meaning soon) and «just now» on Tuesday (meaning later). Which is true? | Both mean not this second | «Now now» means right this second | «Just now» means a minute ago, always | Both mean never
test: «Aziza's 17, she's basically an adult, she'll be fine». What does the lesson say? | A teen doing a parent's jobs needs help too | 17-year-olds never feel sad or tired | Aziza should do even more jobs | Teenagers don't cry about family things
test: «Thandi's sister made her walk behind, so she's ashamed of her». What's more likely? | She wants to look cool in front of her friends | She's ashamed of her sister and always has been | Thandi did something terrible that morning | It's a rule in every South African family
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct of an elder sister who is both domineering and protective? | Both behaviours may proceed from a sense of responsibility | Such conduct evidences animosity towards the younger child | Protective acts are performed solely to impress others | Domineering conduct precludes any protective inclination
exam: Identify the principal consequence, as established in the lesson, of reading another's diary and disclosing its contents. | A grave breach of privacy which may impair trust for a long time | A harmless jest of no lasting effect upon the persons concerned | A reasonable act, given that siblings may hold no secrets | An improvement in relations between the sisters concerned
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a sibling's secret which involves possible danger. | Such a secret ought to be disclosed to a responsible adult | Such a secret must be preserved irrespective of the risk | Such a secret should be communicated to the stranger involved | Such a secret should be published to the sibling's acquaintances
exam: It is given that an elder sister has customarily purchased a beverage for her sibling each week and has ceased to do so for want of funds. State the correct characterisation of the practice. | A gift rather than an obligation | A debt enforceable against the sister | A rule of the household | A payment due under contract
